  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://strategic-advisory.com/ontology/dom/2.0#SPEC_GOVERN_L2.2_L3.5_S06">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://strategic-advisory.com/ontology/dom/2.0#SectionContent"/>
    <dom:belongsToSpecification rdf:resource="http://strategic-advisory.com/ontology/dom/2.0#SPEC_GOVERN_L2.2_L3.5"/>
    <dom:forSection rdf:resource="http://strategic-advisory.com/ontology/dom/2.0#Section_06_DataEconomics"/>
    <dom:textContent>AI governance carries a dual economic role: it is simultaneously a risk cost (compliance investment, governance overhead) and a value enabler (trusted AI commands higher adoption and commercial value). Organisations with mature AI governance frameworks deploy models 40% faster due to reduced rework from late-stage ethical or compliance failures. The cost of AI incidents — reputational, regulatory, and litigation — dwarfs the cost of proactive governance by an order of magnitude.</dom:textContent>
  </rdf:Description>
